    <DataNode elementId="AT2G46370-MONOMER" textLabel="jasmonoyl-amino acid synthetase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9SKE2" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">JAR1 is a jasmonate:amino acid transferase that plays an important role in jasmonic acid metabolism and jasmonic acid signaling in |FRAME: ORG-5993 &quot;&lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;&quot;| |CITS: [15258265] [17291501] [18247047]|. It is structurally related to the acyl adenylate-forming firefly luciferase superfamily of enzymes and it has adenylation activity &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;|CITS:[12084835]|. &lt;i&gt;In vivo&lt;/i&gt;, JAR1 appears to be primarily involved in the production of |FRAME:Jasmonoyl-Isoleucines| (JA-Ile). This compound has been shown to be an active form of jasmonic acid that promotes the interaction between COI1 (an F-box) protein and the JAZ1 repressor protein in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;. In this manner, JA-Ile is believed to help destabilize the JAZ1 repressor and thereby promote JA-responsive transcription |CITS:[17637677]|. &lt;i&gt; In vitro&lt;/i&gt;, JAR1 can catalyze the addition of many different amino acids to jasmonate |CITS: [15258265] [17291501][18247047]|. A few of these JA-amino acid conjugates have been identified in Arabidopsis thaliana, including JA-Val, JA-Leu, JA-Phe, and JA-ACC, but their levels do not change in a jar1 mutant suggesting that other enzymes must catalyze the formation of these products &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [15258265]|. &lt;i&gt;In vitro&lt;/i&gt; analyses of JAR1 show that its Km for isoleucine (0.03 mM), is more than 60-fold lower than its Km for leucine (1.93 mM), phenylalanine (1.84 mM), and valine (2.49 mM) and provide an explanation for the &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; production of JA-Ile by this enzyme. In addition, although JAR1 can act on |FRAME: CPD-5943| and OPC-4 (3-oxo-2-(2Z-pentenyl)-cyclopentane-1-butyric acid) &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;, it shows the highest rate of activity with |FRAME:CPD-734||CITS: [15258265]| . 12-OH-jasmonyl-isoleucine, another JA conjugate identified in Arabidopsis thaliana, can also be formed &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; by JAR1 but the physiological significance of this reaction is unknown |CITS: [17291501]|. There is increasing evidence that different stereoisomers of JA-Ile have very different biological activities. For example, initial studies suggested that |FRAME: CPD-11232 &quot;(-)-JA-Ile&quot;| was a biologically active phytohormone |CITS: [SEMBDER93][17291501]|. However, later work shows that |FRAME: CPD-11259| is the biologically active form of the compound |CITS: [19349968]|. This compound exists at low levels in most commercial preparations of |FRAME: CPD-11232| and may explain the observed biologically activity originally associated with the (-)-JA-Ile stereoisomer. A pure preparation of (-)-JA-Ile does does not promote JAZ/COI1 binding &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [19349968]|. Interestingly, the biologically active form, |FRAME: CPD-11259|, more closely resembles the potent bacterial phytotoxin coronatine than |FRAME: CPD-11232| does |CITS: [19349968][19377448]|. More work will be needed to determine whether JAR1 acts on |FRAME:CPD-731| to generate |FRAME: CPD-11259| or whether this arises from the spontaneous epimerization of |FRAME: CPD-11232| after it has been synthesized by JAR1. Several &lt;i&gt;jar1&lt;/i&gt; mutants have been identified and studied |CITS:[12084835] [18247047] [15258265]|. These mutants demonstrate that JAR1 activity is required for wounding responses |CITS:[18247047]|, protection against ozone damage, resistance to fungi and pathogens, and other jasmonate-related responses (see references in |CITS:[15258265]|). Outside of the realm of jasmonate metabolism, JAR1 can also promote the formation of pA4 (|FRAME: CPD-607|) from tripolyphosphate (|FRAME:P3I|) and ATP &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt;. It therefore remains possible that JAR1 could affect the pools of uncommon nucleoside polyphosphates present in Arabidopsis plants, but, no &lt;i&gt;in vivo&lt;/i&gt; confirmation of this activity has been reported to date |CITS: [17291501]|.</Comment>
